WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.505
The naive ways both failed.

00:00:06.505 --> 00:00:11.009
Serial ran one transaction at a time, correct but far too slow.

00:00:11.009 --> 00:00:15.514
Drop all coordination and the data corrupts.

00:00:15.514 --> 00:00:20.019
The fix is to interleave for speed and still land on a correct result, the same one some one-at-a-time order would give.

00:00:20.019 --> 00:00:24.523
But which interleavings are correct?

00:00:24.523 --> 00:00:29.028
One property answers it, conflict-serializability, and the test is a graph with no cycle.

00:00:29.028 --> 00:00:32.901
First, the vocabulary.

00:00:32.901 --> 00:00:36.774
An action is a single reed or write of one row.

00:00:36.774 --> 00:00:40.647
The notation is compact.

00:00:40.647 --> 00:00:44.520
T1 dot R of A means transaction T1 reeds row A.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:48.394
T1 dot W of A means T1 writes row A.

00:00:48.394 --> 00:00:52.267
And a transaction is just a sequence of these.

00:00:52.267 --> 00:00:56.140
Here, T1 reeds A, then writes A, then reeds B.

00:00:56.140 --> 00:01:00.013
Blue marks a reed, orange marks a write, and the transaction name rides in front, so you always know who did what.

00:01:00.013 --> 00:01:03.886
One rule to remember.

00:01:03.886 --> 00:01:07.759
Correctness tracks only the order of these actions, never the values they compute.

00:01:07.759 --> 00:01:11.563
One swap is easy to judge.

00:01:11.563 --> 00:01:15.367
But a real interleaving makes hundreds of swaps at once, so is the whole thing still correct?

00:01:15.367 --> 00:01:19.170
Three building blocks.

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:22.974
A serial schedule runs one transaction to completion, then the next, with no overlap.

00:01:22.974 --> 00:01:26.778
An interleaved schedule mixes their actions in time, which is faster.

00:01:26.778 --> 00:01:30.582
A serializable schedule is an interleaving whose final state matches what some serial run would have produced.

00:01:30.582 --> 00:01:34.386
That is the prize.

00:01:34.386 --> 00:01:38.190
Serial behavior at interleaved speed.

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:41.993
And notice the word some.

00:01:41.993 --> 00:01:45.797
The database only has to match some valid serial outcome, any one will do.

00:01:45.797 --> 00:01:49.601
So serializable is not the same as serial.

00:01:49.601 --> 00:01:53.475
To judge an interleaving, the database hunts for conflicts.

00:01:53.475 --> 00:01:57.350
A conflict is two transactions, the same row, at least one a write.

00:01:57.350 --> 00:02:01.225
Start with the safe case.

00:02:01.225 --> 00:02:05.099
Two reeds on the same row never conflict, because reeds change nothing.

00:02:05.099 --> 00:02:08.974
The other three pairs all conflict.

00:02:08.974 --> 00:02:12.848
A reed then a write, a write then a reed, or two writes.

00:02:12.848 --> 00:02:16.722
The value an action sees or leaves behind depends on the order.

00:02:16.722 --> 00:02:20.597
That taxonomy is the whole input to the graph.

00:02:20.597 --> 00:02:24.730
Now the test, in three steps.

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:28.863
One, a node for every transaction.

00:02:28.863 --> 00:02:32.996
Two, a directed edge for every conflict, pointing the way the order must go.

00:02:32.996 --> 00:02:37.130
Three, look for a cycle.

00:02:37.130 --> 00:02:41.263
No cycle, and a serial order respects every edge, so the schedule is conflict-serializable.

00:02:41.263 --> 00:02:45.396
A cycle, and two edges demand opposite orders, so no order works, and it is not conflict-serializable.

00:02:45.396 --> 00:02:49.529
Let us run it on a real schedule.

00:02:49.529 --> 00:02:53.650
A concert booking, two counters.

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:57.772
Counter A holds the VIP seats at ten, Counter B the general seats at twenty-two.

00:02:57.772 --> 00:03:01.893
Three transactions book at once, T1, T2, T3.

00:03:01.893 --> 00:03:06.015
The target after they commit, nine and twenty.

00:03:06.015 --> 00:03:09.777
This is your first schedule, so read it with me.

00:03:09.777 --> 00:03:13.538
Each row is one action, in time order, with five columns: who acts, reed or write, which counter, the step, and the value after.

00:03:13.538 --> 00:03:17.300
Now follow it down, one row at a time.

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:21.061
First, T1 reeds Counter A, which holds ten.

00:03:21.061 --> 00:03:24.823
Then T1 writes Counter A down to nine.

00:03:24.823 --> 00:03:28.585
Next, T2 reeds Counter A.

00:03:28.585 --> 00:03:32.346
It reeds Counter B.

00:03:32.346 --> 00:03:36.108
And it writes Counter B to twenty-one.

00:03:36.108 --> 00:03:39.870
Finally, T3 reeds Counter B, then writes it to twenty.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:43.631
Every transaction ran start to finish, so the brackets on the left show three clean blocks.

00:03:43.631 --> 00:03:47.393
That blocked shape is what serial looks like.

00:03:47.393 --> 00:03:51.154
The counters end at nine and twenty, and nothing ran in parallel.

00:03:51.154 --> 00:03:54.916
This is the baseline every interleaving has to beat.

00:03:54.916 --> 00:03:59.111
Even this serial run has conflicts.

00:03:59.111 --> 00:04:03.306
List every pair of actions that touch the same row.

00:04:03.306 --> 00:04:07.501
T1 writes A, then T2 reeds it, an edge from T1 to T2.

00:04:07.501 --> 00:04:11.696
T2 writes B, then T3 writes it, an edge from T2 to T3.

00:04:11.696 --> 00:04:15.891
The conflict graph from those edges has no cycle.

00:04:15.891 --> 00:04:20.086
Topological sort hands back T1, then T2, then T3, exactly the order we ran.

00:04:20.086 --> 00:04:23.680
Now interleave them.

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:27.275
Schedule S1 prime mixes actions from all three transactions in time, hopping between them.

00:04:27.275 --> 00:04:30.869
Same actions as the serial run, just shuffled.

00:04:30.869 --> 00:04:34.464
It also lands on A equals nine, B equals twenty.

00:04:34.464 --> 00:04:38.058
The question is whether the shuffle broke anything.

00:04:38.058 --> 00:04:42.019
Build the conflict graph for S1 prime.

00:04:42.019 --> 00:04:45.979
Here T2 touches A before T1 does, so the edge runs from T2 to T1, the opposite direction from the serial run.

00:04:45.979 --> 00:04:49.940
T2 still writes B before T3, an edge from T2 to T3.

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:53.901
The graph has no cycle.

00:04:53.901 --> 00:04:57.862
Topological sort gives a valid serial order, T2, then T1, then T3.

00:04:57.862 --> 00:05:01.822
Conflict-serializable.

00:05:01.822 --> 00:05:05.783
Faster than serial, still provably safe.

00:05:05.783 --> 00:05:10.829
Schedule S2 prime is the same three transactions under a different interleaving.

00:05:10.829 --> 00:05:15.876
It adds a second write on B and reorders the actions in a way that looks reasonable.

00:05:15.876 --> 00:05:20.922
To see whether it is safe, build its conflict graph and watch the shape.

00:05:20.922 --> 00:05:24.890
The conflict graph for S2 prime starts the same way.

00:05:24.890 --> 00:05:28.858
T2 grabs A before T1, an edge from T2 to T1.

00:05:28.858 --> 00:05:32.826
T2 writes B before T3 reeds it, an edge from T2 to T3.

00:05:32.826 --> 00:05:36.794
But now watch.

00:05:36.794 --> 00:05:40.762
T3 writes B before T2 reeds it later, an edge from T3 to T2.

00:05:40.762 --> 00:05:44.730
T2 must come before T3, and T3 must come before T2.

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:48.698
That is a cycle.

00:05:48.698 --> 00:05:52.666
No serial order satisfies both, so S2 prime is not conflict-serializable.

00:05:52.666 --> 00:05:56.634
The shuffle broke the one thing the serial run had for free, a single consistent order.

00:05:56.634 --> 00:05:59.919
You have watched the test rule on two schedules.

00:05:59.919 --> 00:06:03.204
Now see why it works.

00:06:03.204 --> 00:06:06.489
Take this small interleaved schedule, and look at T1, in violet.

00:06:06.489 --> 00:06:09.773
Its two actions sit apart, with a T2 action wedged between them.

00:06:09.773 --> 00:06:13.058
The whole goal is to swap neighbours and slide T1's actions together into one block, then T2's into the next.

00:06:13.058 --> 00:06:16.343
That is a serial schedule.

00:06:16.343 --> 00:06:19.628
The catch.

00:06:19.628 --> 00:06:22.913
You can only swap a pair that does not conflict.

00:06:22.913 --> 00:06:26.198
Check the neighbouring pairs here.

00:06:26.198 --> 00:06:29.483
The middle pair touches different rows, so it swaps freely.

00:06:29.483 --> 00:06:32.768
Make that one swap, and T1 is one block, T2 is the next.

00:06:32.768 --> 00:06:36.052
Serial, T1 then T2.

00:06:36.052 --> 00:06:39.337
So the rule is simple.

00:06:39.337 --> 00:06:42.622
A schedule slides to serial exactly when its conflict graph has no cycle.

00:06:42.622 --> 00:06:45.907
A cycle is a knot the swaps can never undo.

00:06:45.907 --> 00:06:50.868
Step back and see the full landscape of schedules.

00:06:50.868 --> 00:06:55.830
The outermost ring holds every serializable schedule, anything equivalent to some serial run.

00:06:55.830 --> 00:07:00.791
Inside it sits the conflict-serializable class, the one our cycle test draws.

00:07:00.791 --> 00:07:05.752
Smaller still are the locking protocols built next in this section, starting with two-phase locking.

00:07:05.752 --> 00:07:10.714
And tighter again, strict two-phase locking.

00:07:10.714 --> 00:07:15.675
Each ring sits strictly inside the next, so a protocol only ever produces schedules already known safe.

00:07:15.675 --> 00:07:18.703
Conflict-serializability is the gold standard for a correct interleaving.

00:07:18.703 --> 00:07:21.731
We now know what correct means.

00:07:21.731 --> 00:07:24.758
How does a database deliver it while staying fast?

00:07:24.758 --> 00:07:27.786
A lock manager.

00:07:27.786 --> 00:07:30.814
Next, the locks stack and the lock manager.
